    "result_text": "The full suite is green \u2014 **30 tests, 0 failures**, including the daily-limit test `Cp19Tests`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the per-day owner-creation cap as an OfficeFloor pipeline step, mirroring the existing city-capacity rule.\n\n**New files:**\n- `CheckOwnerDailyCreateLimit.java` (function) \u2014 counts owners whose `registrationDate` equals today via `OwnerRepository.findAll()`; throws `OwnerDailyCreateLimitException` when the count is \u2265 100.\n- `OwnerDailyCreateLimitException.java` \u2014 checked exception (`extends Exception`) so it surfaces in the `throws` clause.\n- `OwnerDailyCreateLimitExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 responds `429 TOO_MANY_REQUESTS` with a `ProblemDetail` body, using the shared `ProblemDetails` builder.\n- `officefloor/escalation/\u2026OwnerDailyCreateLimitException.yml` \u2014 maps the exception to the handler.\n\n**Wired in:**\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted a `checkDailyCreateLimit` step (under `transaction` governance) between `checkCityCapacity` and `build`, so an over-limit request is rejected before the owner is constructed.\n\nThe rule reuses the same \"count via `findAll()` before build\" approach as the city-capacity check, and owners default their `registrationDate` to the current date in `BuildOwner`, so the count reflects today's registrations. The pre-existing `Cp19Tests` (create 100, expect the 101st \u2192 429) passes.",
